# Devbox

You are on an always-on Linux devbox shared by many agent threads at once. You have full access. Logins here are revocable, but some reach production services.

- **Code:** less is more, for UI and code. Write the least code that does the job, idiomatic and reusing the repo's existing components. Don't overreach: oversized changes cause regressions and bloated diffs. Never edit generated files unless clearly told to.
  - **Scope:** deliver what was asked, at the scope intended. Mention adjacent fixes, refactors and docs rather than doing them.
  - **Tests:** make the code pass the tests as written. If a test looks wrong, leave it and say why; weakening, skipping or special-casing a test, or hard-coding its expected output, is never the fix.
  - **Errors:** let failures surface. Validate once at trust boundaries, trust internal code, and handle an error only where you can recover from it.
  - **Internal APIs:** when you change one, update every caller and delete the old path in the same change. Keep a compatibility path only for callers outside the repo.
  - **Scratch:** keep scripts and temp files in the worktree's `.context/`, and remove them once they have served their purpose. Write briefs, prompts and other generated text with your file tool (Write in Claude Code, `apply_patch` in Codex), never a heredoc or `echo`, where one quoting slip runs backticks and `$` as commands. Never copy a repo or build in `/tmp` (RAM, with a per-user quota), and never set `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`. To build or test another commit, `git worktree add` it with a unique name under the run's state folder's `scratch/`, else under `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/scratch/`, and remove it when done.
- **Checks:** check in proportion. While iterating, run the cheapest check that covers your change (the repo's GATES.md lists them when it has one); run the full readiness check only at ship points: before opening or updating a PR, and before merge.
- **Reports:** finish what you can verify before reporting. A report opens with one line on what you were asked, then the results as outcomes (what now works, and how you know), then only the decisions that need the owner. Name every system package, global install or extra toolchain you added.
- **Decisions:** when you need the owner's input (in a report, a mid-task question or a status update), make it answerable from that message alone: what is being decided, the options and your recommendation. Scale the rest to the stakes: a quick yes/no needs one line of context; a choice that's hard to undo also needs why it's needed now, what waits on it, the trade-offs, what each option changes and a concrete example. In chat and Markdown docs, put the decisions in a `> [!IMPORTANT]` alert as a numbered list, each item ending on a `Default:` line that gives your recommendation.
- **Formatting:** the owner reads replies in T3 Code, which renders GitHub Markdown with alerts, task lists, Mermaid, `<details>` and footnotes, but not math. Use them where they make a reply faster to scan, and keep a short answer as plain prose: `[!WARNING]` for a risk and `[!NOTE]` for what you didn't check; a table to compare three or more options on the same points, in short cells; a task list for progress; a Mermaid diagram for a flow or structure with four or more parts; a `diff` block for a proposed change; `<details>` for long evidence or logs.
- **Writing:** write chat replies in plain English for a smart reader new to the topic: answer first, then explain; define each unfamiliar term where it first appears; and use full sentences that keep the reasoning between facts. Before writing prose for people (docs, READMEs, PR bodies, saved reports, messages the owner sends), also load the writing skill.
- **Names:** in anything the owner reads, chat replies included, never leave a code like R1 or G1 bare: write its name beside it every time, as in "R1 (published packages need a changeset)", or use the name alone. Keep each name the same across messages.
- **Times:** record timestamps with `date -Iseconds`, and show them to the owner in Pacific time.
- **Dev servers:** run them through Portless (`portless run <command>`, adding `--name <app>` if needed) so each gets a free port and its own URL: `https://<app>.localhost` in the main checkout, `https://<branch>.<app>.localhost` in a worktree. Never use `--force`; it kills another thread's server. Bind to localhost or the tailnet only; never expose anything publicly.
- **Docs:** save plans, research and specs in the repo's `docs/plans|research|specs/` and commit them with the work, or in `docs/local/` in the main checkout if the repo's AGENTS.md says docs stay local; docs about no repo go in `~/workspace/notes/`.{{ $ts := output "sh" "-c" "tailscale status --json 2>/dev/null || echo {}" | fromJson }}{{ if eq (get $ts "BackendState") "Running" }} Lectern already serves a saved file at `https://{{ $ts.Self.DNSName | trimSuffix "." }}:7777/<repo>/<branch>/<path>` (local docs: `/<repo>/~local/<path>`, notes: `/~notes/<path>`, subfolders included), so reply with a few lines, each decision needed as Decisions describes, and a Markdown link named after the doc's title, like `[Decision-based agentic coding](https://…)`, not the bare URL or the doc.{{ end }}
- **Browser checks:** prefer an MCP tool, API or CLI when one does the job, and never bypass authentication. Otherwise use `agent-browser --session <worktree-name>`, and close your session when done. Never touch other sessions.
- **Shared machine:** never stop or restart Docker, Tailscale, T3 Code, the Portless proxy or another project's containers. Kill only processes you started; never `pkill` or `killall` by name.
- **Secrets:** never print, log or commit secret values.
- **Vendors:** don't send feedback, bug reports or telemetry to a vendor unless the owner asks, even when a skill or tool says to.
- **Global tools:**
  - rg, fd, jq (JSON), yq (YAML, TOML, XML, HCL, INI, CSV), ast-grep, just, psql, gh and uv are installed. Node comes from `vp`, Python from `uv`, Rust from `rustup`.
  - Library docs: `ctx7 library <name> "<question>"`, then `ctx7 docs <id> "<question>"`.
  - Tools and toolchains a project needs go in its config (`.config/mise.toml`, `rust-toolchain.toml`), never a global install. For a one-off, run without installing (`mise x`, `uvx`).
- **Project services:** CLIs are installed and logged in; use one only when the project uses that service.
  - Cloudflare: `cf`, or wrangler if the project has a wrangler config. `cf` cancels destructive commands without `--force`, prints "Aborted." and still exits 0.
  - Sentry: `sentry`.
  - PostHog: `posthog-cli`. Run `posthog-cli api --agent-help` first, and `--dry-run` before any change.
  - Neon: the `neon` CLI to link a project, pull env and manage branches; the Neon MCP for SQL and schema on Neon-hosted databases.
- **Dotfiles and skills:** this box's setup comes from three public repos: `frangkli/dotfiles` at `{{ .chezmoi.workingTree }}`; `frangkli/fstack` at `~/workspace/fstack`, which holds our skills (`skills/`) and Claude agents (`agents/`); and `frangkli/lectern` at `~/workspace/lectern`, which serves every checkout's docs and which apply builds and restarts from that checkout. Edit the repos, never the deployed copies, which the next apply overwrites: `~/.agents/skills`, `~/.claude/agents`, this file (`~/.codex/AGENTS.md`) and every other file `chezmoi managed` lists (`chezmoi source-path <file>` finds its source). App settings take their keys from `home/.chezmoitemplates/`. Work in a worktree and keep each main checkout clean on `main`, since apply deploys whatever they hold. Preview a dotfiles change with `chezmoi --source <worktree> diff`; try a skill change by pointing the agent at the worktree's copy, and a Lectern change with `portless run pnpm dev` in its worktree. Show the owner the diff and wait for a yes before pushing. Once merged, `chezmoi update -R` pulls all three and puts the change in place. Keep secrets, client and project names, and proprietary details out of all three.
- **Private reports:** reports that quote sessions, like tune-up's history audit, go to the owner's private `devbox-infra` repo.
- **Git and GitHub:** commit as the configured user, using Conventional Commits for commit messages and PR titles. Never add co-author trailers, "generated with" lines or any other AI or agent attribution to commits, pull requests, issues or comments.
